<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hurrians</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hurrian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hurrians were a people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>, speaking a language unrelated to Semitic or Indo-European tongues, who were widespread in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. They formed the main population of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni Empire</a>, ruled by an Indo-Aryan elite. Hurrian religion and culture also strongly influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Some historians believe the Hurrians originated in the Armenian highlands or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> and migrated south into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fertile-crescent">Fertile Crescent</a>. Several scholars have proposed the Kura-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> culture as their ancestor. Excavations at Tell Mozan, ancient Urkesh, show Hurrian artefacts with affinities to Kura-Araxes, though Antonio Sagona finds a connection hard to demonstrate. Others suggest the Hurrians were native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, linked to the prehistoric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-halaf">Tell Halaf</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a> cultures, while Igor Diakonoff thought them newcomers who arrived in the third millennium BCE. Their language is agglutinative and related to Urartian, together forming the Hurro-Urartian family; wider connections are disputed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur River</a> valley became the heart of Hurrian lands for a millennium. The first known Hurrian kingdom arose around Urkesh in the third millennium BCE. The king Tupkish had a queen named Uqnitum, Akkadian for &quot;girl of lapis lazuli&quot;, and there is evidence that the Hurrians were allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> by the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a>. By 1725 BCE they were found in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, at places such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a>. The mixed Amorite-Hurrian kingdom of Yamhad struggled with the early Hittite king Hattusilis I around 1600 BCE, and Hurrians settled in Kizzuwatna in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><p>The Hurrians who entered northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> at the end of the 17th century BCE brought political and cultural change, establishing feudal states led by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a>-warrior aristocracy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni Empire</a>, which first rose to power before 1550 BCE, was bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> to the north, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> to the south-west, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassites">Kassites</a> to the south-east and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> to the east. Egyptian records of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-i">Thutmose I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thutmose-iii">Thutmose III</a> mention it, usually as Naharin. After an internal succession crisis Mitanni fell to the Hittites and later came under Assyrian control. The kingdom of Shupria, north of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, survived until Assyria conquered it in 673-672 BCE, and by the Early Iron Age the Hurrians had been assimilated into other peoples.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Knowledge of Hurrian culture comes from excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuzi">Nuzi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets, mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusa">Hattusa</a>, the Hittite capital. The Hurrians adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-language">Akkadian language</a> and cuneiform script about 2000 BCE. They were skilled potters, and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khabur-river">Khabur</a> ware and Nuzi ware were valued as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. They may have been the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> to recognise the full potential of the horse, and with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> the first in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> to use the horse-drawn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> in war. Hurrian texts from Ugarit hold the oldest known written music, from about 1400 BCE.</p><p>Their religion influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>, spreading from the cult centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kummanni">Kummanni</a> in Kizzuwatna. Teshub was the weather god and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebat">Hebat</a> his wife; Kumarbi was the father of Teshub. The Hurrian myth The Songs of Ullikummi, preserved among the Hittites, parallels <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesiod">Hesiod</a>'s Theogony.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hurrians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Religious and literary traditions absorbed from the Hattians and <b>Hurrians</b> were transmitted westward, and the Hurro-Hittite Kumarbi cycle, with its succession of warring sky gods, closely parallels Hesiod's Theogony, one of the earliest documented channels of cultural transmission between the Near East and Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>By the mid-3rd millennium BCE it was the main political and religious centre of the <b>Hurrians</b>, a people who spoke a language unrelated to the Semitic tongues of their neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/state/urkesh">Urkesh</a></li></ul></section>
